[block
  'ORD-0025-chains'
  [ctx
    'the spec of EMS-002'
    '.guidelines/.orders/EMS-002/EMS-002.pgml'
  [/ctx]
  [tgt
    'a known command after an operand, a space or a closing bracket is a chain link, by the inclusion the Regent decided on 2026-10-25; an ORD of the batch the version moves once for'
  [/tgt]
  [ctx
    'measured at b827782: a dash makes a chain link only right after a command name with nothing buffered; after an operand, after a space or after a closing bracket the dash and the name fall into prose, and the command never reaches the tree; the grammar already reads a chain item as a name or a literal, so a dash after a literal continues the chain, and the lexer broke its own grammar'
  [/ctx]
  [ctx
    'the rules that include it: the chained_command production of glyph-grammar.ebnf; GLOSSARY section 0.1, a chain is the commands applied to the same operand; GLOSSARY section 0.3 normative, in a sequence the subject of the second is the result of the first; and both inverses already write a link after a closed command, so the way back already expected it'
  [/ctx]
  [cnst
    'a dash becomes the extend operator when the text before it is empty or a space, the token before it is an open, a bare tag, a literal or a closing bracket, and a name the vocabulary knows touches it; the lexer stays independent of options; prose such as an arrow, a spaced minus or a hyphenated word stays prose'
  [/cnst]
  [cnst
    'after a closing bracket the link goes to the scope that bracket returns to, the enclosing command or the block, read as GLOSSARY section 0.3 sequence; the way back fences an off element whose prose starts with a dash and a known name, so the reader never invents a link the document did not carry'
  [/cnst]
  [cnst
    'no declared source reaches it, so no hash of the snapshot moves; the LK bucket, a D-07 probe and the parse probes hold it, and the Rust is ported byte for byte'
  [/cnst]
  [cmp
    'the links'
    'the three positions keep the command as a chain link in the tree, the XML, the burn and both ways back, prose stays prose, and fenced prose comes back as prose; each seen failing before it counts, and the Rust answers byte for byte'
    [ctx[/ctx]
    [cnst[/cnst]
  [/cmp]
  [sub
    [eq
      [cmp
        [ctx[/ctx]
      [/cmp]
    [/eq]
  [/sub]
  [elab
    [eq[/eq]
    [sub[/sub]
    [rsn[/rsn]
  [/elab]
  [ref
    [cnst[/cnst]
  [/ref]
  [ctx[/ctx]
  [core[/core]
  [core[/core]
